Q: Explain your understanding of TCP/IP, HTTP, and Internet protocols
(JD line: “understanding of TCP/IP, HTTP, and Internet protocols”)

────────────────────────────────────────
One-liner

Internet communication is layered: **IP** routes packets, **TCP** delivers reliable streams, **HTTP** is the application protocol
your REST APIs use on top (usually over TCP, often with TLS as HTTPS).

────────────────────────────────────────
1) Big picture — OSI vs TCP/IP (interview view)

Simplified TCP/IP stack:

| Layer        | Examples                         | Job                          |
| ------------ | -------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| Application  | HTTP, HTTPS, DNS, SMTP, FTP, SSH | What apps speak              |
| Transport    | TCP, UDP                         | Process-to-process delivery  |
| Internet     | IP, ICMP                         | Host addressing & routing    |
| Link/Network | Ethernet, Wi-Fi                  | Local frame delivery         |

Your Spring Boot API:
Browser/App → HTTP(S) → TCP → IP → network → reverse path to Tomcat/Netty

────────────────────────────────────────
2) IP (Internet Protocol)

- Addresses devices: IPv4 (`192.168.1.10`) / IPv6
- Routes **packets** hop by hop (best-effort — can be lost/reordered)
- Does **not** guarantee delivery by itself

IPv4 vs IPv6 (quick):
- IPv4 = 32-bit, e.g. `192.168.1.10` (scarce → NAT common)
- IPv6 = 128-bit, e.g. `2001:db8::1` (huge space)
Details: `IPv4 vs IPv6`

Related:
- DNS maps `api.example.com` → IP (see `DNS — Domain Name System`)
- Private vs public IPs, NAT in real networks

────────────────────────────────────────
3) TCP (Transmission Control Protocol)

Reliable, connection-oriented transport.

Features:
- Connection setup (3-way handshake: SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK)
  Simple explanation: `TCP 3-way handshake explained simply`
- Guaranteed ordered delivery (retransmit lost segments)
- Flow control / congestion control
- Ports identify apps (e.g. `443`, `8080`)

Used by: HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, HTTPS, SMTP, SSH, most databases clients, Kafka (TCP), etc.

When you say “API on port 8080” → TCP port on the server process.

────────────────────────────────────────
4) UDP (contrast — know this)

Unreliable, connectionless, lower latency.
No handshake / no automatic retransmission.

Used by: DNS queries (often), video/voice, gaming, QUIC (HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP).

See also: `TCP vs UDP`

────────────────────────────────────────
5) HTTP (HyperText Transfer Protocol)

Application-layer protocol for request/response between client and server.

Classic request:
GET /users/1 HTTP/1.1
Host: api.example.com
Authorization: Bearer ...

Response:
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json

{ "id": 1, "name": "Afham" }

Key ideas for backend interviews:
- Methods: GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE (idempotency matters)
- Status codes: 2xx success, 4xx client, 5xx server
- Headers: Content-Type, Authorization, Cache-Control, Cookie
- Stateless: each request independent (sessions/JWT add app state)
- HTTPS = HTTP + TLS encryption (certificates)

────────────────────────────────────────
❓ Are HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 the same as HTTP and HTTPS?

NO — different questions.

1) HTTP/1.1 vs HTTP/2 = **version** of HTTP (how the protocol works)
2) HTTP vs HTTPS = **with or without TLS** (encrypted or not)

Think of a 2D grid:

|                | No TLS (http://)     | With TLS (https://)      |
| -------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------ |
| HTTP/1.1       | possible             | common                   |
| HTTP/2         | rare (h2c)           | very common in browsers  |
| HTTP/3         | —                    | over QUIC (UDP)          |

So:
- `https://api.com` can be HTTP/1.1 **or** HTTP/2 (both are HTTPS)
- HTTP/2 is still HTTP — newer, faster framing (multiplexing, binary, header compression)
- HTTPS is not a separate “version”; it means HTTP wrapped with TLS

Examples:
- http://localhost:8080  → HTTP/1.1 (typical Spring local), no TLS
- https://google.com     → usually HTTP/2 (or 3) over TLS

Interview one-liner:
“1.1/2/3 are HTTP versions; HTTP vs HTTPS is whether TLS encryption is used.”

Details: can also remember from your performance notes (HTTP/2 enable in Spring).

────────────────────────────────────────
❓ Is TCP/TLS the only difference between HTTP and HTTPS?

Short answer:
**Difference between HTTP and HTTPS = TLS (encryption), NOT TCP.**

Both HTTP and HTTPS (HTTP/1.1 & HTTP/2) usually run on **TCP**.

|                   | HTTP                             | HTTPS |
| -                 | ----                         | ----- |
| Application protocol | Same HTTP messages | Same HTTP messages |
| Transport (typical)  | TCP                | TCP |
| Encryption            | No (plain text)   | Yes — **TLS** wrapper TLS (encrypt + certificate)|
| Default port          | 80                | 443 |
| Certificates          | No                | Yes (server identity) |

So:
HTTPS ≈ HTTP + TLS (+ usually still TCP underneath)

NOT:
HTTPS ≠ “HTTP but with TCP”   ← wrong (HTTP already uses TCP)

Flow:
HTTP:  App → HTTP → TCP → IP
HTTPS: App → HTTP → TLS → TCP → IP

What TLS adds:
- Encryption (others can’t read body/headers easily)
- Integrity (tamper detection)
- Server authentication (certificate)

Nuance:
HTTP/3 uses QUIC over **UDP** (still can be encrypted) — so “HTTPS always TCP” is the common interview answer for HTTP/1.1/2, with HTTP/3 as exception.

Related in your Spring notes:
- REST GET conventions
- CSRF (cookie auto-send)
- JWT Bearer header

────────────────────────────────────────
6) Other internet protocols you should name

| Protocol | Role |
| -------- | ---- |
| HTTPS/TLS | Encrypt HTTP |
| DNS | Name → IP |
| SMTP | Email send |
| FTP/SFTP | File transfer |
| ICMP | Ping / network diagnostics |
| WebSocket | Full-duplex over HTTP upgrade |
| HTTP/2 | Multiplexing, binary framing (often on TLS) |
| HTTP/3 | HTTP over QUIC (UDP-based) |

────────────────────────────────────────
7) End-to-end path (say this aloud)

1. Client resolves host via **DNS** → IP  
2. Opens **TCP** connection (handshake) to server:port  
3. Optional **TLS** handshake (HTTPS)  
4. Sends **HTTP** request  
5. Spring DispatcherServlet / controller handles it  
6. HTTP response back over same TCP connection (keep-alive possible)  
7. Connection eventually closes  

────────────────────────────────────────
8) Backend-relevant gotchas

- Connection reset / timeouts → TCP/network path issues
- Load balancer terminates TLS then forwards HTTP internally
- HTTP keep-alive reuses TCP connections
- gRPC usually HTTP/2 over TCP
- Firewall rules allow TCP ports (80/443/8080)

────────────────────────────────────────
Interview answer (45–60 sec)

“I understand internet communication as layered protocols. IP handles addressing and routing of packets. TCP provides a reliable,
 ordered byte stream between client and server using ports and a handshake.
 HTTP sits on top as the request/response protocol for APIs — methods, headers, status codes — and HTTPS adds TLS.
 Supporting protocols I use daily include DNS for name resolution and tools like WebSockets or HTTP/2 where needed.
 In Spring Boot terms, a REST call is HTTP over TCP/IP to our embedded server.”

